<!--Parser Documentation — AttributeClassification
  FQN: org.blockml.bml.compiler.parser.documentation.AttributeClassification-->
<pardocumentation:AttributeClassification xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:parcap="org.blockml.bml.compiler.parser.capability"
  xmlns:pardocumentation="org.blockml.bml.compiler.parser.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    Fixed priority chain for XML attributes (Rules 1–3)
  </is>
  <documentation>
    When mapping an XML element, every attribute is classified — first matching rule wins:
    
    Rule 1 — XML infrastructure:
    • xmlns* → SourceInfo.namespaceBindings (incl. xmlns:xsi — B25)
    • xsi:schemaLocation / xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation → SourceInfo (B25)
    • other xsi:* → ignore (not memberValues)
    
    Rule 2 — bml:* attributes:
    • Composition meta (bml:id, bml:version, bml:generics, …) → BlockInstance fields
    • Directives (bml:each, bml:if, …) → directives[]
    • Unknown bml:* → BML_UNKNOWN_DIRECTIVE (error at map time — B2)
    
    Rule 3 — Grammar vs instance:
    • On definition/grammar nodes, remaining attributes are closed. Unknown → BML_UNKNOWN_ATTRIBUTE (structural error); preserved as unknownAttributes.
    • On block instances, remaining attributes → memberValues[name] (camelCase); ${ expr } → ExpressionValue { raw } (B4). Unknown members are a validator error.
    
    Serialize side of Rule 1 (B25 re-emit) → @blockml/serializer.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="pardocumentation:StructuralGrammar" relation="ReadNext" />
    <see type="parcap:ParseDirectives" relation="SeeAlso" />
  </references>
</pardocumentation:AttributeClassification>
